WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:06.200
Drivine is a graph database client, for
Kotlin and Java, TypeScript and Python.

2
00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:11.640
It works with graph databases like Neo4j,
FalkorDB and Memgraph, and with relational

3
00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:14.920
databases like Postgres,
Oracle and SQLite.

4
00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:16.360
It works at two levels.

5
00:00:16.855 --> 00:00:21.415
The persistence manager runs your queries,
written in Cypher, even on a relational

6
00:00:21.415 --> 00:00:22.415
database.

7
00:00:22.535 --> 00:00:25.975
And the stateless graph object manager
maps your graph to objects.

8
00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:30.200
But it's not a traditional
object graph mapper.

9
00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:34.520
Those start from one big model of your
domain, and generate queries from it.

10
00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:39.240
The model doesn't fit every use case, so you
fetch too much, or too little, and

11
00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:40.240
it's slow.

12
00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:45.265
Drivine takes its cue from GraphQL, and
from composition over inheritance.

13
00:00:45.505 --> 00:00:50.705
You compose small, reusable fragments into a
view for each use case, and Drivine

14
00:00:50.705 --> 00:00:52.465
loads it in a single statement.

15
00:00:52.625 --> 00:00:53.665
That's why it's fast.

16
00:00:55.193 --> 00:00:56.793
So why a graph at all?

17
00:00:56.873 --> 00:01:00.953
A graph lets you think in networks, and
bring graph data science to them.

18
00:01:01.353 --> 00:01:03.353
And it's a great fit for AI apps.

19
00:01:03.353 --> 00:01:06.313
Take retrieval augmented
generation, or RAG.

20
00:01:06.473 --> 00:01:10.158
Before a language model answers, you search
your own documents for the passages

21
00:01:10.158 --> 00:01:13.758
closest to the question, and give
them to the model as context.

22
00:01:14.238 --> 00:01:18.878
Usually those passages are chunks in a vector
store, each cut off from its document,

23
00:01:18.878 --> 00:01:21.278
its neighbours, and the
things it talks about.

24
00:01:21.678 --> 00:01:24.613
So the model gets fragments,
without their context.

25
00:01:25.013 --> 00:01:27.813
In a graph, a match comes with
what it's connected to.

26
00:01:27.973 --> 00:01:29.493
That's graph RAG.

27
00:01:29.493 --> 00:01:33.594
But it usually means picking a graph
database first, and staying on it.

28
00:01:33.594 --> 00:01:37.914
Drivine aims to give you the cutting-edge
experience of working in the graph paradigm,

29
00:01:37.914 --> 00:01:40.474
with whatever database you have, in
the language you work in.

30
00:01:42.073 --> 00:01:45.913
Here's graph RAG as Embabel does
it, in its graph RAG module.

31
00:01:45.913 --> 00:01:48.873
Embabel is an agent framework for the JVM.

32
00:01:48.873 --> 00:01:52.953
A chunk is a fragment, with a vector index,
and a view adds what it's connected

33
00:01:52.953 --> 00:01:53.953
to.

34
00:01:53.953 --> 00:01:58.368
The stateless graph object manager loads the
nearest chunks by vector, with everything

35
00:01:58.368 --> 00:02:00.128
the view names, in one statement.

36
00:02:00.608 --> 00:02:04.768
Essentially the whole of Embabel's graph RAG
uses the graph object manager, and

37
00:02:04.768 --> 00:02:07.488
Drivine takes care of writing the
queries and statements:

38
00:02:07.648 --> 00:02:12.528
in each platform's flavour of Cypher, or
in SQL or SQL/PGQ, as needed.

39
00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:16.060
The database is configuration.

40
00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:22.860
The same code runs on Neo4j, FalkorDB and
Memgraph, and on Postgres, Oracle and

41
00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:25.820
SQLite, which don't run Cypher themselves.

42
00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:30.533
There, Drivine keeps the graph in
two tables and writes SQL.

43
00:02:30.933 --> 00:02:34.053
Move from one to another, and
nothing in your code changes.

44
00:02:35.593 --> 00:02:39.993
You can write plain openCypher on any of
these stores, even the relational ones:

45
00:02:40.233 --> 00:02:45.673
on Postgres, Oracle and SQLite, Drivine
compiles it to SQL, and gives back the

46
00:02:45.673 --> 00:02:47.273
same rows as a graph engine.

47
00:02:47.673 --> 00:02:49.833
But the object manager saves you time:

48
00:02:49.993 --> 00:02:54.084
it uses your types, writes the
queries, and maps the results.

49
00:02:54.324 --> 00:02:58.164
So use it wherever it makes your life
easier, and fall back to Cypher for your

50
00:02:58.164 --> 00:02:59.164
custom queries.

51
00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:01.820
Here it is running.

52
00:03:01.820 --> 00:03:06.140
Guide is an Embabel agent that answers
questions from Embabel's own documentation.

53
00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:10.940
Its store is built on Drivine's stateless
graph object manager, and runs on any

54
00:03:10.940 --> 00:03:12.060
of the supported databases.

55
00:03:12.867 --> 00:03:16.227
Could you show me what, an
agent looks like?

56
00:03:16.227 --> 00:03:19.907
Show me some code in Kotlin for
a simple Embabel agent.

57
00:03:19.907 --> 00:03:22.867
Here's a simple Embabel agent
written in Kotlin.

58
00:03:22.867 --> 00:03:24.307
Jasper for a horoscope.

59
00:03:24.307 --> 00:03:28.047
The agent Graph RAG is one way
graphs help an AI app.

60
00:03:28.207 --> 00:03:32.127
You can also build an agent's memory and
conversation history, knowledge graphs

61
00:03:32.127 --> 00:03:34.767
it can reason over, and the
domain model it works in.

62
00:03:35.087 --> 00:03:39.712
For example, Embabel's chat store keeps an
agent's conversations, with a vector

63
00:03:39.712 --> 00:03:44.272
for every message, on any of the
supported databases, through Drivine.

64
00:03:44.272 --> 00:03:48.352
And DICE builds knowledge graphs of
propositions an agent reasons over, on any

65
00:03:48.352 --> 00:03:49.952
of Drivine's supported databases.

66
00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:56.580
Drivine also ships with Studio, an optional tool
that comes in handy during development.

67
00:03:57.060 --> 00:04:02.100
Run ad hoc Cypher, or load your Drivine models
and see what each view brings back.

68
00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:06.340
Drivine is open source, for Kotlin and
Java, TypeScript and Python.

69
00:04:06.955 --> 00:04:10.875
Build AI apps on the database you already
run, and keep the freedom to move.

70
00:04:10.875 --> 00:04:15.851
Drivine is the database integration layer
for Embabel, the AI agent framework

71
00:04:15.851 --> 00:04:20.651
for the JVM, created by Rod Johnson, who
also created the Spring Framework.

72
00:04:20.651 --> 00:04:23.131
Drivine was created by Jasper Blues.

